    "result_text": "Done. The one test class in the suite passes and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec called for de-duplicating a new owner's `customerCode` when the computed `<REGION>-<HASH8>` collides with an existing owner's code \u2014 appending `-<n>` with the smallest `n \u2265 2` that makes it unique.\n\nI split the change along responsibility lines:\n\n- **`CustomerCode.java`** \u2014 added a pure `deduplicate(String code, Set<String> taken)` helper next to the existing `format`/`region` methods. It returns the code unchanged when free, otherwise tries `code-2`, `code-3`, \u2026 and returns the first not in `taken`. Keeping this with the other customer-code string logic means the collision rule lives with the code that mints codes, and it's trivially unit-testable in isolation.\n\n- **`AssignCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 the pipeline step now takes the `OwnerRepository` (injected by type, as `CountNamesakes` already does), gathers the set of existing owners' customer codes, computes the base code, and runs it through `CustomerCode.deduplicate(...)`. The step keeps its single job \u2014 assigning the code before save \u2014 and delegates the uniqueness rule to the helper rather than growing its own.\n\nNo YAML wiring changed: `AssignCustomerCode` already runs after telephone normalization and before `save` in `owners.POST.yml`, so the new owner isn't yet persisted when it queries existing codes \u2014 exactly the \"state just prior to this create\" semantics the neighbouring `CountNamesakes` step relies on.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreCustomerCodesStayUnique` passes (distinct owners get distinct codes), and `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nOne thing worth flagging (not part of this change): the OpenAPI spec at `src/main/resources/openapi.yml` still documents `customerCode` as `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (e.g. `LON-SMI-0007`), which is out of sync with the actual `<REGION>-<HASH8>` implementation. Let me know if you'd like me to reconcile that.",
